{"conjecture": "Every software change embeds an implicit insurance premium against unobservable state dependencies—execution paths, timing orderings, and environmental couplings that no test suite or reviewer can see at change time. These premiums compound multiplicatively rather than additively, so after N changes the effective cost of a safe modification scales as (1+p)^N, not 1+Np, because each new hidden dependency can interact with all prior hidden dependencies. This explains why systems become catastrophically rigid even when every individual change appeared locally correct: the premium is unobservable at deposit time but compounds into structural lock-in."}
